  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Budgeting for a home build in Texas means planning for the whole project, not just the construction contract: land, site work, permits, utilities, design, financing costs and a real contingency. Get those on paper before you break ground, track every dollar as you go, and most overruns become small adjustments instead of crises. Here is how to build a budget that holds up.</p>

<h2>Start with what you can actually afford</h2>
<p>Work backward from a monthly payment you are comfortable with, not the maximum a lender will approve. Include property taxes, which can be significant in many Texas counties, homeowners insurance, HOA dues if any, and expected utilities. Then talk to a lender about construction or construction-to-permanent financing so you know your down payment and reserve requirements. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-finance-building-a-house/">how to finance building a house</a> explains the loan types.</p>

<h2>Know every line item before you break ground</h2>
<p>A complete budget usually includes these buckets:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Land</strong>: purchase price, closing costs, survey, and any soil or percolation testing.</li>
<li><strong>Soft costs</strong>: plans or architect fees, engineering (foundation design is especially important on Texas expansive clay soils), permits and any impact or tap fees.</li>
<li><strong>Site work</strong>: clearing, grading, drainage, driveway, and utility connections, or a well and septic system on rural land.</li>
<li><strong>Construction</strong>: foundation, framing, roofing, MEP (mechanical, electrical, plumbing), insulation, drywall, finishes.</li>
<li><strong>Finishing and move-in</strong>: appliances, window coverings, fencing, landscaping and irrigation.</li>
<li><strong>Financing costs</strong>: construction loan interest during the build, loan fees and inspections tied to draws.</li>
<li><strong>Contingency</strong>: money set aside for what nobody can predict.</li>
</ol>
<p>Costs vary widely by city, lot and finish level, so get local quotes rather than relying on online averages. For a breakdown of the ranges people typically see, read <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-house-in-texas/">how much it costs to build a house in Texas</a>.</p>
<p>If you want to understand how pros price a house, an [[amz:books_estimating|estimating guide for residential construction]] teaches you to read and check bids line by line.</p>

<h2>Set a contingency and protect it</h2>
<p>A contingency of around 10 percent of construction cost is a common starting point. Push it toward 15 to 20 percent if your plans are not final, the lot is raw land, or you are acting as your own general contractor. Then treat it as untouchable for upgrades: it exists for rock under the slab, a material price jump, or a failed inspection, not for a fancier backsplash.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Keep a running "contingency remaining" number at the top of your budget sheet. Every time you approve a change order, subtract it immediately. Seeing that number shrink is the best brake on impulse upgrades.</p></div>

<h2>Spend where it counts</h2>
<h3>Separate needs from wants</h3>
<p>Write two lists before you meet with a builder: must-haves and nice-to-haves. Must-haves are things that are expensive or impossible to change later, like the number of bedrooms, ceiling heights, the foundation, insulation and window quality. Nice-to-haves are often easy to add later: upgraded light fixtures, a fancier faucet, an outdoor kitchen.</p>
<p>Spend on the bones first. You can swap a countertop in ten years; you cannot easily add attic insulation under a vaulted ceiling or move a load-bearing wall.</p>

<h3>Choose materials for value, not just price</h3>
<p>The cheapest option is not always the best value. In the Texas climate, money spent on the building envelope often pays you back every month:</p>
<ul>
<li>Good attic insulation and a radiant barrier</li>
<li>Low-E windows sized and placed with sun exposure in mind</li>
<li>Ductwork sealed and, where possible, inside conditioned space</li>
<li>A right-sized HVAC system based on a load calculation</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/energy-efficient-home-building-in-the-texas-heat/">energy-efficient building in the Texas heat</a> goes deeper. Where you save is on finishes you can upgrade later: builder-grade lighting, standard cabinet hardware, durable laminate or luxury vinyl plank instead of hardwood in high-traffic areas.</p>

<h2>Lock in selections and bids early</h2>
<p>Most budget blowups come from decisions made late. Finalize plans and as many selections as possible before construction starts. Get written bids that spell out what is included and excluded, and watch out for "allowances" that are set unrealistically low. If a bid says "$X allowance for lighting," ask what that actually buys.</p>
<p>When you hire trades yourself, compare at least three bids per scope. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-hire-and-manage-subcontractors/">hiring and managing subcontractors</a> covers what a good bid should contain.</p>

<h2>Track spending and stay involved</h2>
<h3>Update your numbers every week</h3>
<p>A budget only works if you compare it to reality. Each week, update:</p>
<ul>
<li>Budgeted amount per line item</li>
<li>Committed amount (signed contracts and purchase orders)</li>
<li>Paid to date</li>
<li>Variance and contingency remaining</li>
</ul>
<p>A spreadsheet works, but plenty of owner-builders like having a paper record on site too. A [[amz:home_planner|house-building planner]] keeps selections, bids and payments together, and a [[amz:document_organizer|document organizer]] holds receipts, lien waivers and draw paperwork.</p>
<!--products:home_planner,document_organizer,books_estimating|Tools that make budget tracking easier-->

<h3>Communicate and document</h3>
<p>Visit the site regularly, ask questions early, and put decisions in writing. A quick text confirming "we agreed to move the outlet, no charge" can save an argument later. Ask your builder or lender how draws are inspected so you only pay for completed work, and collect lien waivers as you pay subcontractors and suppliers.</p>

<h3>Plan for the end of the job</h3>
<p>Budgets often hold up until the last month, then leak away on punch-list items, landscaping the HOA requires, blinds, and a dozen trips to the hardware store. Keep a line for these move-in costs and do not dip into it early.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Turn this into a real number for your project: start with our <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-house-in-texas/">Texas build cost guide</a>, then map the schedule with the <a href="/new-home-construction-timeline/">new home construction timeline</a>, since time is money on a construction loan. The <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> shows how owner-builders plan budgets and keep control from start to finish.</p>
